WEBVTT

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:15.280
shall never forget the weekend laura died a silver sun burned through the sky like a huge magnifying

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:24.980
gas it was the hottest sunday in my recollection felt as if i were the only human being left in new

00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:29.510
york for laura's horrible death

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:55.990
I, Waldo Lidecker, was the only one who really knew her, and I had just begun to write Laura's story when another of those detectives came to see me. I had him wait. I could watch him through the half-open door.

00:02:10.040 --> 00:02:13.140
I noted that his attention was fixed upon my clock.

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:16.410
There was only one other in existence,

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:19.010
and that was in Laura's apartment,

00:02:19.010 --> 00:02:24.290
the very room where she was murdered.

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:29.220
Careful there. That stuff is priceless.

00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:40.850
- Come in here, please. - Mr. Lidecker?

00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:44.880
Oh, you recognize me. I'm splendid. Sit down, please.

00:02:48.570 --> 00:02:53.240
- Nice little place you have here, Mr. Lidecker. - It's lavish, but I call it home.

00:02:54.210 --> 00:02:56.870
I suppose you'll hear about the Laura Hunt murder.

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:00.000
Yesterday morning, after Laura's body was found, I was

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:06.730
question by Sergeants McAvity and Schultz. And I stated, on Friday night, Laura had a dinner

00:03:06.730 --> 00:03:13.100
engagement with me, after which she was ostensibly going out of town. She phoned and canceled our

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:19.500
engagement at exactly seven o'clock. After that, I... You ate a lonely dinner, then got into the tub to

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:30.000
read. Why did you write it down? Afraid you'd forget it? I am the most widely misquoted man in America, and my friend

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:34.250
From Sergeants McAvery and Schultz, I shall find it intolerable.

00:03:34.330 --> 00:03:40.970
Hand me that washcloth, please, Mr. - Mr. McPherson.

00:03:41.050 --> 00:03:44.020
- McPherson. - McPherson.

00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:47.520
- Mark McPherson, the siege of Babylon, Long Island.

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:49.920
The gangster with the machine gun killed three policemen.

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:52.400
I told the story over the air and wrote a column about it.

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:54.400
Are you the one with the leg full of lead?

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:57.190
- The man who walked right in and got him? - Yeah.

00:03:57.270 --> 00:04:00.000
Well, well. Hand me my robe for you.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:10.700
- You have a pretty good memory, Mr. Leidecker. - I always liked that detective with a silver

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:15.400
shinbone. - Thanks. I hope you won't have any reason to change your mind about me.

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:21.510
- Have you any more questions? - Yeah, just one. Two years ago, in your October 17th column,

00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:25.240
you started out to write a book review, but at the bottom of the column, you switched

00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:30.000
over to the Harrington murder case. - Are the processes of the creative mind now on

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:35.000
the jurisdiction of the police. You said Harrington was rubbed out with a shotgun loaded with buckshot.

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:39.990
The way Laura Hunt was murdered, night before last. Did I?

00:04:39.990 --> 00:04:45.090
Yeah, but he was really killed with a sash weight. How ordinary.

00:04:45.090 --> 00:04:49.980
My version was obviously superior. I never bother with details, you know.

00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:55.120
I do. Well, so long. Mind if I go with you?

00:04:55.120 --> 00:05:00.000
What for? Murder is my favorite crime.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:05.430
I write about it regularly, and I know you'll have to visit everyone on your list of suspects.

00:05:06.820 --> 00:05:08.170
I'd like to study their reactions.

00:05:09.020 --> 00:05:10.360
You're on the list yourself, you know.

00:05:10.580 --> 00:05:13.770
Good. To have overlooked me would have been a pointed insult.

00:05:15.220 --> 00:05:18.200
You're not the sort of man one would insult, Mr. Lydekker.

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:20.210
Do you really suspect me?

00:05:20.350 --> 00:05:20.710
Yes.

00:05:22.550 --> 00:05:26.440
McPherson, if you know anything about faces, look at mine.

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:30.000
How singularly innocent.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:34.860
Have you ever seen such candid eyes?

00:05:34.940 --> 00:05:41.350
Something you confiscated in a raid on the kindergarten?

00:05:41.430 --> 00:05:43.550
It takes a lot of control.

00:05:43.630 --> 00:05:47.260
- Would you like to try it? - No, thanks.

00:05:47.340 --> 00:05:51.110
Were you in love with Laura Hunt, Mr. Lidegger?

00:05:51.190 --> 00:05:55.870
Is she in love with you?

00:05:55.950 --> 00:06:00.000
Laura considered me the wisest, the wittiest.

00:06:00.030 --> 00:06:01.610
the most interesting man she'd ever met.

00:06:02.490 --> 00:06:04.780
I was in complete accord with her on that point.

00:06:06.900 --> 00:06:09.660
She thought me also the kindest, the gentlest,

00:06:10.330 --> 00:06:12.940
the most sympathetic man in the world.

00:06:13.540 --> 00:06:14.950
Did you agree with her there too?

00:06:16.310 --> 00:06:18.410
McPherson, you won't understand this,

00:06:19.970 --> 00:06:23.340
but I tried to become the kindest, gentlest,

00:06:24.170 --> 00:06:26.150
the most sympathetic man in the world.

00:06:26.740 --> 00:06:27.590
Have any luck?

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:30.000
Let me put it this way.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:36.000
I should be sincerely sorry to see my neighbor's children devoured by wolves.

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:37.940
Shall we go?

00:07:03.260 --> 00:07:05.370
- Good morning, Lieutenant. - Good morning, Mrs. Treadwell.

00:07:05.450 --> 00:07:08.410
- Good morning, Anne. - I've just taken over the Laura Hunt case.

00:07:08.420 --> 00:07:10.980
- Would you sit down? - Thanks.

00:07:10.980 --> 00:07:14.870
I have all the reports, but there are a few more questions I'd like to ask.

00:07:14.950 --> 00:07:16.830
Why, certainly. I'll do anything I can to help.

00:07:16.910 --> 00:07:20.580
- You were fond of your niece, Mrs. Treadwell? - Why, I adored her.

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:21.940
- A cigarette? - Thanks.

00:07:22.020 --> 00:07:24.060
You collapsed when you identified the body.

00:07:24.140 --> 00:07:25.440
You can quite understand that.

00:07:25.520 --> 00:07:28.290
Shotgun loaded with buckshot, close range.

00:07:28.330 --> 00:07:30.000
Not very nice to look at.

00:07:30.410 --> 00:07:35.710
horrible. Her maid Bessie, I suppose she was devoted to Miss Hunt. Oh, she worshipped her. Laura

00:07:35.710 --> 00:07:41.790
had had her for years. I'll never forget her scream when she saw Laura lying there. Did you

00:07:41.790 --> 00:07:49.360
approve of Miss Hunt's coming marriage to Mr. Carpenter? Why? Shouldn't I approve? I don't know.

00:07:50.040 --> 00:08:00.000
What is your relationship with Mr. Carpenter? What do you mean? What I mean is he's been a

00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:02.140
This is beginning to assume fabulous aspects.

00:08:02.140 --> 00:08:05.090
Oh, shut up, Waldo. What are you driving at?

00:08:05.090 --> 00:08:06.980
The truth, Mrs. Dreadwell. Are you in love with him?

00:08:06.980 --> 00:08:14.840
I know. I'm very fond of Mr. Carpenter, of course. Everybody is.

00:08:14.840 --> 00:08:16.840
I'm not. I'll be hanged if I am.

00:08:16.840 --> 00:08:18.840
Oh, don't be so annoying, Waldo.

00:08:18.840 --> 00:08:21.210
Did you give Mr. Carpenter money?

00:08:21.210 --> 00:08:22.450
What do you mean?

00:08:22.450 --> 00:08:25.790
A couple of checks went through your account endorsed by him.

00:08:25.790 --> 00:08:30.000
One on May 15th for $250, one on May 22nd for $50.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:35.670
Oh, that. I asked him to do some shopping for me, that's all.

00:08:35.670 --> 00:08:37.670
Now, Shelby's a very obliging fellow.

00:08:37.670 --> 00:08:41.750
For some time also, you've been withdrawing various amounts in cash.

00:08:41.750 --> 00:08:47.300
Sometimes 1,500, sometimes 1,700 at a clip.

00:08:47.300 --> 00:08:49.880
Yes, I needed that money.

00:08:49.880 --> 00:08:55.630
The day you took out 1,500 dollars, Mr. Carpenter deposited 1,350.

00:08:55.630 --> 00:09:00.000
When you withdrew 1,700, he deposited 1,500.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:04.770
- Maybe they were shooting crap. - Oh, must I be insulted like this?

00:09:04.870 --> 00:09:11.020
I'm sorry, Mrs. Straderill, but I have to find out about these things.

00:09:11.120 --> 00:09:14.740
Shelby needed some money and I lent it to him, that's all.

00:09:14.840 --> 00:09:17.730
Well, after all, it is my money. I suppose I can do as I'm pleased with it.

00:09:17.830 --> 00:09:21.570
Of course. Now, on Friday night, Mrs. Straderill, you stayed home alone all evening.

00:09:21.670 --> 00:09:24.840
- Yes. - Why didn't you go to the concert with Shelby?

00:09:24.940 --> 00:09:30.000
- Because he didn't ask me. - We were just talking about you, Captain.

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:32.140
What a coincidence to find you here.

00:09:32.180 --> 00:09:35.140
- This is Lieutenant McPherson. - Oh, how do you do, Lieutenant?

00:09:35.180 --> 00:09:37.700
I didn't know you were here, Mr. Carpenter.

00:09:37.740 --> 00:09:39.700
Well, as a matter of fact, I was just lying down here.

00:09:39.740 --> 00:09:43.700
My hotel room was so hot, and then all the people and reporters and telephone,

00:09:43.740 --> 00:09:48.010
you know how it is, Lieutenant. I've hardly slept a wink since it happened.

00:09:48.050 --> 00:09:50.670
Is that a sign of guilt or innocence, McPherson?

00:09:50.710 --> 00:09:55.040
I'm at your disposal, Lieutenant. I'm as eager to find the murderer as you are.

00:09:55.080 --> 00:09:58.040
But what possible motive could I have for killing Laura?

00:09:58.080 --> 00:10:00.000
Miss Hunt and I were going to be married this week,

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:04.960
- No, he doesn't know. And neither do I, no you or anyone else alive. - What do you mean by that?

00:10:04.960 --> 00:10:12.790
Laura had not definitely made up her mind to marry him. She told me so herself last Friday when she called up to cancel our dinner engagement.

00:10:12.790 --> 00:10:22.750
As a matter of fact, she was going to the country to think it over. She was extremely kind, but I was always sure she would never have thrown her life away on a male beauty in distress.

00:10:22.750 --> 00:10:30.000
I suppose you've heard losers whine before, especially in your profession, eh? Would you like a bite of lunch?

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:32.920
- Now, that's very thoughtful. The perfect host. - Waldo.

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:34.880
You'd almost think he was in his own home.

00:10:34.960 --> 00:10:38.340
Shelby knows how distracted I am. Would you, Lieutenant?

00:10:38.420 --> 00:10:40.820
Thank you, Mrs. Treadwell, but I've got to be going.

00:10:40.900 --> 00:10:42.130
But, Lieutenant, I...

00:10:42.160 --> 00:10:44.810
Well, I rather thought you'd want to ask me some questions.

00:10:44.890 --> 00:10:48.110
Oh, yes. What did they play at the concert Friday night?

00:10:48.190 --> 00:10:50.650
Oh, Brahms' First and Beethoven's Night.

00:10:50.730 --> 00:10:54.420
Uh-huh. Have you got a key to Miss Hunt's house up in the country?

00:10:54.500 --> 00:10:56.820
No, but I think there's one up in her apartment.

00:10:56.900 --> 00:10:59.560
- Okay, well, have a look. - Perhaps I could help you?

00:11:00.670 --> 00:11:02.670
All right, come along.

00:11:02.670 --> 00:11:04.040
I'll be seeing you, Mrs. Treadwell.

00:11:57.520 --> 00:11:58.450
The bell rang.

00:12:00.030 --> 00:12:03.350
And she opened the door, the shop was fired.

00:12:03.350 --> 00:12:05.550
- And how do you deduce that?

00:12:05.550 --> 00:12:09.880
- Trunk backwards, I was there.

00:12:09.880 --> 00:12:12.900
- I thought you hadn't been up here before.

00:12:12.900 --> 00:12:19.370
- I saw the police thought of it.

00:12:19.370 --> 00:12:28.220
- Yes, I'd better try and find that key.

00:12:28.220 --> 00:12:30.000
- McPherson, tell me, why did they have to

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:31.910
photograph her in that horrible condition.

00:12:31.910 --> 00:12:32.990
- When a dame gets killed,

00:12:32.990 --> 00:12:34.490
she doesn't worry about how she looks.

00:12:34.490 --> 00:12:36.850
- Will you stop calling her a dame?

00:12:36.850 --> 00:12:39.020
Look around.

00:12:39.020 --> 00:12:43.200
Is this the home of a dame?

00:12:43.200 --> 00:12:48.920
Look at her.

00:12:48.920 --> 00:12:50.680
- Not bad.

00:12:50.680 --> 00:12:53.790
- Jacoby was in love with her when he painted it,

00:12:53.790 --> 00:12:57.890
but he never captured her vibrance, her warmth.

00:12:57.890 --> 00:12:59.160
Have you ever been in love?

00:13:00.440 --> 00:13:03.400
A doll in Washington Heights once got a fox fur out of me.

00:13:03.480 --> 00:13:06.080
Ever know a woman who wasn't a doll or a dame?

00:13:06.120 --> 00:13:10.050
Yeah, one.

00:13:10.760 --> 00:13:14.560
But she kept walking me past furniture windows to look at the parlor suites.

00:13:15.520 --> 00:13:17.410
Would you mind turning that off?

00:13:17.450 --> 00:13:18.730
Why don't you like it?

00:13:18.890 --> 00:13:20.600
It was one of Laura's favorites.

00:13:20.660 --> 00:13:23.010
Not exactly classical, but sweet.

00:13:23.100 --> 00:13:24.430
You know a lot about music?

00:13:24.510 --> 00:13:27.880
I don't know a lot about anything, but I know a little about practically everything.

00:13:27.980 --> 00:13:28.740
Yeah?

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:34.970
Why did you say they played Brahms first and Beethoven's ninth at the concert Friday night?

00:13:34.970 --> 00:13:37.710
They changed the program at the last minute and played nothing but Sibelius.

00:13:37.710 --> 00:13:40.090
Well, I suppose I should have told you in the first place.

00:13:40.090 --> 00:13:43.090
You see, I'd been working on that advertising campaign with Laura.

00:13:43.090 --> 00:13:46.430
Well, we'd been working so hard, I just couldn't keep my eyes open.

00:13:46.430 --> 00:13:49.080
I didn't hear a note at the concert. I fell asleep.

00:13:49.080 --> 00:13:52.080
Next, he'll produce photographic evidence of his dreams.

00:13:52.080 --> 00:13:55.240
I know it sounds suspicious, but I'm resigned to that by now.

00:13:55.240 --> 00:13:58.820
I'm a natural-born suspect just because I'm not the conventional type.

00:13:58.820 --> 00:14:00.000
I wouldn't worry about it.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:01.180
Mr. Carpenter.

00:14:01.180 --> 00:14:03.010
Sounds reasonable.

00:14:03.010 --> 00:14:04.610
I fall asleep at concerts myself.

00:14:04.610 --> 00:14:08.030
Thank you.

00:14:08.030 --> 00:14:09.030
You found that key yet?

00:14:09.030 --> 00:14:11.030
No, I looked for it in the den, but it wasn't there.

00:14:11.030 --> 00:14:18.010
It may be in here.

00:14:18.010 --> 00:14:22.440
Here it is.

00:14:22.440 --> 00:14:25.680
I knew there must be one around somewhere.

00:14:25.680 --> 00:14:28.730
The police are very fussy about their inventories.

00:14:28.730 --> 00:14:30.000
That key isn't on the list of things.

00:14:30.160 --> 00:14:31.760
that were in that drawer yesterday.

00:14:32.300 --> 00:14:36.820
- Then it's made a recent reappearance. - You put it there, didn't you?

00:14:37.430 --> 00:14:39.270
- Yes. - Why?

00:14:39.730 --> 00:14:42.430
- It's just that I didn't want to give it to you while Waldo was present. - Oh.

00:14:42.530 --> 00:14:44.880
I have private reasons that don't concern him.

00:14:45.080 --> 00:14:47.250
Everything about Laura concerns me.

00:14:47.790 --> 00:14:51.480
You have private reasons, no doubt, to lie about the key.

00:14:51.580 --> 00:14:54.620
Waldo, for your own good, I'm warning you to stop implying

00:14:54.720 --> 00:14:56.920
that I had anything to do with Laura's death.

00:14:57.800 --> 00:14:59.760
Very well, I'll stop implying.

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:01.440
and make a direct statement.

00:15:01.460 --> 00:15:04.390
- All right, you ask one. - I wouldn't.

00:15:05.200 --> 00:15:08.940
Will you please stop dawdling with that infernal puzzle?

00:15:08.990 --> 00:15:12.790
- It's getting on my nerves. - I know, but it keeps me calm.

00:15:12.870 --> 00:15:14.490
Okay, let's go.

00:15:30.990 --> 00:15:34.130
This is our table, Laura's and mine.

00:15:36.640 --> 00:15:38.320
We spent many quiet evenings here together.

00:15:41.220 --> 00:15:44.010
Remember we dined here the night before her 22nd birthday.

00:15:45.860 --> 00:15:51.100
Just we two, happy, making plans for her future.

00:15:53.810 --> 00:16:00.000
But this was a far cry from the girl who walked into my life, the Algonquin Hotel, five years

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:24.480
before. Excuse me, Becca, do. My name is Laura Hunt, and I'm with Fullerton Company, you

00:16:24.480 --> 00:16:29.160
know, the big advertising firm. And I'd like to talk something over with you, if I may.

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:33.600
I hardly fail to realize that I'm engaged in eating my lunch.

00:16:33.700 --> 00:16:37.430
Yes, I know. I'm awfully sorry to interrupt this way.

00:16:37.530 --> 00:16:40.450
But it's so hard to get to see you the regular way.

00:16:40.550 --> 00:16:43.260
And this will only take a minute, really.

00:16:43.360 --> 00:16:46.830
Now, this is--

00:16:46.930 --> 00:16:52.340
Young woman, either you have been raised in some incredibly rustic community...

00:16:52.440 --> 00:16:54.420
where good manners are unknown...

00:16:54.480 --> 00:16:57.070
or you suffer from the common feminine delusion...

00:16:57.170 --> 00:17:00.000
that the mere fact of being a woman exempts you from the root...

00:17:00.220 --> 00:17:04.460
of civilized conduct, or possibly both.

00:17:04.550 --> 00:17:07.760
Possibly, but here's what I wanted to show you.

00:17:07.860 --> 00:17:10.060
It's for the Wallace Flo-Write pen.

00:17:10.160 --> 00:17:12.620
I know my company would be glad to pay you $5,000

00:17:12.720 --> 00:17:14.320
if you'll endorse the ad.

00:17:14.340 --> 00:17:15.540
I don't use a pen.

00:17:15.640 --> 00:17:18.300
I'll write with a goose quill dipped in venom.

00:17:18.340 --> 00:17:21.820
Yes, but this is a very fine pen, Mr. Lidecker.

00:17:21.920 --> 00:17:23.420
The best on the market.

00:17:23.520 --> 00:17:25.640
Wouldn't you at least consider endorsing it?

00:17:25.700 --> 00:17:30.000
I'll either consider, endorse, or use the Wallace pen.

00:17:30.760 --> 00:17:32.470
I hate pens.

00:17:33.200 --> 00:17:36.310
If your employers wish me to publish that statement in my column,

00:17:36.780 --> 00:17:39.890
you may tell them that I should be delighted to oblige.

00:17:40.140 --> 00:17:43.330
Oh, no. You mustn't do that.

00:17:43.630 --> 00:17:46.060
Don't blame Bulletin Company, Mr. Lidecker.

00:17:46.250 --> 00:17:49.460
They don't know anything about this. It was my idea to see you.

00:17:51.550 --> 00:17:53.600
- Indeed? - Yes.

00:17:53.730 --> 00:17:56.890
I know they'd give anything down at the office to get your endorsement.

00:17:56.990 --> 00:17:59.730
Only they think there's no use asking. So...

00:18:00.380 --> 00:18:05.140
I had this ad made up all on my own because I thought, well, what's the harm in trying?

00:18:05.660 --> 00:18:07.840
There was always a chance that you might, Mr. Lidecker.

00:18:08.480 --> 00:18:10.210
Just think what it would mean to...

00:18:10.210 --> 00:18:13.710
You seem to be completely disregarding something more important than your career.

00:18:14.550 --> 00:18:15.020
What?

00:18:15.880 --> 00:18:16.920
My lunch.

00:18:17.740 --> 00:18:21.180
I don't really believe that.

00:18:22.270 --> 00:18:22.750
Implicitly.

00:18:23.470 --> 00:18:26.630
I never heard of anything so selfish.

00:18:28.180 --> 00:18:30.000
In my case, self-absorption is kind of...

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:34.690
I have never discovered any other subject quite so worthy of my attention.

00:18:34.690 --> 00:18:41.150
But you write about people with such real understanding and sentiment.

00:18:41.150 --> 00:18:45.150
That's what makes your column so good.

00:18:45.150 --> 00:18:48.660
The sentiment comes easily at 50 cents a word.

00:18:48.660 --> 00:18:54.690
Well, if that's the way you really feel, you must be very lonely.

00:18:54.690 --> 00:18:58.950
Will you kindly continue this character and Alice's elsewhere?

00:19:00.540 --> 00:19:01.540
You begin to bore me.

00:19:01.540 --> 00:19:03.840
You're a poor man.

00:19:03.840 --> 00:19:09.140
I'm very sorry for you.

00:19:09.140 --> 00:19:11.140
Actually, I was annoyed by the incident.

00:19:11.140 --> 00:19:14.380
But she had something about her, that girl.

00:19:14.380 --> 00:19:17.740
I had to speak to her again.

00:19:17.740 --> 00:19:21.050
I had to see her.

00:19:21.050 --> 00:19:23.050
- Miss, would you mind if I... - Just a moment, please.

00:19:23.050 --> 00:19:25.050
I'll tell Mr. Ballett right away. He's on the telephone.

00:19:25.050 --> 00:19:26.050
Thank you.

00:19:48.300 --> 00:19:51.690
Boy, Waldo Lydecker to see Miss Laura Hunt.

00:19:52.340 --> 00:19:54.420
Announce me.

00:19:56.700 --> 00:19:58.900
Johnny, please tell the gentleman I'm busy.

00:20:03.870 --> 00:20:07.590
Ms. Hunt, I have something to say to you.

00:20:07.730 --> 00:20:09.480
You've already said it, Mr. Lydekker.

00:20:10.320 --> 00:20:13.640
I wish to point out that you caught me at my most difficult moment.

00:20:14.320 --> 00:20:16.680
Ordinarily, I am not without a heart.

00:20:16.900 --> 00:20:17.900
Really?

00:20:18.810 --> 00:20:21.210
Shall I produce X-ray pictures to prove it?

00:20:24.400 --> 00:20:25.950
I wish to apologize.

00:20:26.630 --> 00:20:28.710
Your apology is accepted.

00:20:31.470 --> 00:20:33.530
Goodbye, Mr. Lidecker.

00:20:33.530 --> 00:20:35.690
If you come a little bit closer, my boy,

00:20:35.690 --> 00:20:38.070
I can just crack your skull with my stick.

00:20:40.660 --> 00:20:46.580
And now, for reasons which are too embarrassing to mention,

00:20:46.580 --> 00:20:49.680
I'd like to endorse that pen.

00:20:49.680 --> 00:20:56.380
Mr. Lidecker, thank you.

00:20:56.380 --> 00:21:00.000
- You're a very strange man. - What?

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:03.670
- I'm really sorry for the way you acted, aren't you?

00:21:03.670 --> 00:21:06.280
- Let's not be psychiatric, Miss Hunt.

00:21:06.280 --> 00:21:10.520
But in a word, yes.

00:21:10.520 --> 00:21:12.450
- It's very kind of you, you know.

00:21:12.450 --> 00:21:14.010
- I'm not kind, I'm vicious.

00:21:14.010 --> 00:21:18.740
It's the secret of my charm.

00:21:18.740 --> 00:21:22.100
But if you choose to think me kind,

00:21:22.100 --> 00:21:25.770
I'll call for you at six.

00:21:25.770 --> 00:21:29.020
All right? - All right.

00:21:34.120 --> 00:21:36.960
Her career began with my endorsement.

00:21:36.960 --> 00:21:40.040
I secured other endorsements for her,

00:21:40.040 --> 00:21:43.840
introduced her to important clients.

00:21:43.840 --> 00:21:47.410
I gave her her start, but it was her own talent

00:21:47.410 --> 00:21:49.490
and imagination that enabled her to rise

00:21:49.490 --> 00:21:54.140
to the top of her profession and stay there.

00:21:54.140 --> 00:21:57.810
She had an eager mind always.

00:21:57.810 --> 00:22:00.000
She was always quick to seize upon anything

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:04.830
but improve her mind, her appearance.

00:22:04.830 --> 00:22:12.020
Laura had innate breeding.

00:22:12.020 --> 00:22:16.060
She deferred to my judgment and taste.

00:22:16.060 --> 00:22:21.850
I selected a more attractive hairdress for her.

00:22:21.850 --> 00:22:27.010
I taught her what clothes were more becoming to her.

00:22:27.010 --> 00:22:29.090
Through me, she met everyone.

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:37.830
famous and the infamous. Her youth and beauty, her poise and charm of manner captivated them

00:22:37.830 --> 00:22:49.090
all. She had warmth, vitality. She had authentic magnetism. Wherever we went, she stood out.

00:22:50.330 --> 00:22:57.570
Men admired her. Women envied her. She became as well-known as Waldo Lydica's walking stick

00:22:57.570 --> 00:22:59.530
and his white carnation.

00:23:00.800 --> 00:23:03.790
But Tuesday and Friday nights, we stayed home,

00:23:03.790 --> 00:23:08.420
dining quietly, listening to my records.

00:23:08.420 --> 00:23:11.080
I read my articles to her.

00:23:11.080 --> 00:23:15.820
The way she listened was more eloquent than speech.

00:23:15.820 --> 00:23:20.040
These were the best nights.

00:23:20.040 --> 00:23:24.030
Then one Tuesday, she phoned and said she couldn't come.

00:23:24.030 --> 00:23:27.160
It didn't matter, really.

00:23:27.160 --> 00:23:30.000
But when it happened again the following Friday,

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:34.300
- I was disturbed.

00:23:34.300 --> 00:23:36.270
- I didn't understand it.

00:23:36.270 --> 00:23:38.530
I felt betrayed.

00:23:38.530 --> 00:23:48.080
And yet I knew Laura would never betray anyone.

00:23:48.080 --> 00:23:52.400
I walked for a long time.

00:23:52.400 --> 00:23:56.090
Then I found myself before her apartment building.

00:23:56.090 --> 00:23:57.800
The lights were on.

00:23:57.800 --> 00:23:59.570
It pleased me to know she was home.

00:24:00.640 --> 00:24:05.620
Till I saw she was not alone, but I waited.

00:24:06.790 --> 00:24:08.570
I wanted to see who he was.

00:24:12.490 --> 00:24:16.530
It was Jacoby, who had recently painted her portrait.

00:24:17.500 --> 00:24:19.070
I never liked the man.

00:24:20.060 --> 00:24:23.740
He was so obviously conscious of looking more like an athlete than an artist.

00:24:24.800 --> 00:24:27.580
I sat up the rest of the night writing a column about him.

00:24:28.440 --> 00:24:30.000
I demolished his affectations.

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:35.110
Exposed his camouflaged imitations of better painters, ridiculed his theories.

00:24:35.110 --> 00:24:39.910
I did it for her, knowing Jacoby was unworthy of her.

00:24:39.910 --> 00:24:42.740
It was a masterpiece, because it was a labor of love.

00:24:42.740 --> 00:24:46.140
Naturally, she could never regard him seriously again.

00:24:46.140 --> 00:24:49.500
There were others, of course.

00:24:49.500 --> 00:24:55.770
But her own discrimination ruled them out before it became necessary for me to intercede.

00:24:55.770 --> 00:25:00.000
Until one night at a party at Ann Pelley.

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:05.410
Treadwell. It was one of her usual round-ups of bizarre and nondescript characters,

00:25:05.490 --> 00:25:07.830
chorale from every stratum of society.

00:25:07.910 --> 00:25:11.210
This is Mr. and Mrs. Preston. They've been waiting all evening to meet you.

00:25:11.290 --> 00:25:16.530
- How do you do? - Where Shelby?

00:25:16.610 --> 00:25:23.240
Excuse me, honey. You're Laura Hunt.

00:25:23.890 --> 00:25:28.930
- Yes. - Hello. I'm Shelby Carpenter. Want to dance?

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:34.710
Oh, him? I bet he's still doing the poker.

00:25:34.710 --> 00:25:36.710
Excuse me, please.

00:25:36.710 --> 00:25:39.410
Yes, Betsy Ross taught her to me.

00:25:39.410 --> 00:25:42.410
Hello, old girl. Darling, how are you?

00:25:42.410 --> 00:25:44.410
I see you've met Shelby.

00:25:44.410 --> 00:25:45.410
Unavoidably.

00:25:45.410 --> 00:25:47.410
He was awfully nice to me in Louisville at the Derby.

00:25:47.410 --> 00:25:49.410
His family's from Kentucky.

00:25:49.410 --> 00:25:51.900
Sharecroppers, no doubt.

00:25:56.900 --> 00:26:00.000
Louise, Louise, for the last time, will you miss?

00:26:00.160 --> 00:26:02.160
I won't. But I saved some chicken livers for you.

00:26:02.160 --> 00:26:03.160
Oh, you're an angel.

00:26:03.160 --> 00:26:06.160
In the meantime, darling, you think you could get this spot out for me?

00:26:06.160 --> 00:26:09.050
I can afford a blemish on my character, but not on my clothes.

00:26:09.050 --> 00:26:11.050
I couldn't eat another mouth.

00:26:11.050 --> 00:26:12.870
I'm afraid of cruise.

00:26:12.870 --> 00:26:14.540
I'm afraid of liquor.

00:26:14.540 --> 00:26:16.520
Oh, may I have a glass of milk from Mr. Lidecker?

00:26:16.520 --> 00:26:17.520
Of course, Miss Hunter.

00:26:17.520 --> 00:26:19.520
I've got to tell you, I also read palms.

00:26:19.520 --> 00:26:23.520
I cook, I swallow swords, I mend my own socks and never eat garlic or onions.

00:26:23.520 --> 00:26:25.520
What more can you want of a man?

00:26:25.520 --> 00:26:26.640
Don't listen to that Scallyway.

00:26:26.640 --> 00:26:28.650
I didn't expect to find him here, Louise.

00:26:28.650 --> 00:26:30.000
Well, what do you mean?

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:34.840
we're old friends. She feeds me, humors me, repairs me, and refuses to marry me, don't

00:26:34.840 --> 00:26:39.350
you, honey? I do. She has good sense. Wait just a minute. Thanks, Louise. You're wasting

00:26:39.350 --> 00:26:46.290
your time. She's got good sense, too. You're jealous. What does it feel like, Mr. Carpenter?

00:26:46.940 --> 00:26:53.740
What does what feel like, Miss Hunt? Living on the income from an estate. Well, I, uh... Or don't

00:26:53.740 --> 00:27:00.000
you know? Well, I did until the sheriff took it over ten years ago. Why maintain the

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:01.000
Why not work?

00:27:01.000 --> 00:27:04.000
Believe it or not, I asked one of my many friends for a job once.

00:27:04.000 --> 00:27:07.000
Executive of a big company, 2,500 employees.

00:27:07.000 --> 00:27:10.000
He could have pressed the button and done it, but he just laughed.

00:27:10.000 --> 00:27:11.000
He thought I was joking.

00:27:11.000 --> 00:27:11.950
Weren't you?

00:27:11.950 --> 00:27:12.950
No.

00:27:12.950 --> 00:27:13.950
When I convinced him, he got embarrassed.

00:27:13.950 --> 00:27:14.950
Said he'd phone me.

00:27:14.950 --> 00:27:15.950
That was months ago.

00:27:15.950 --> 00:27:19.030
Now, whenever he sees me, he looks the other way.

00:27:21.100 --> 00:27:28.840
Do you really want a job?

00:27:33.360 --> 00:27:34.440
Oh, here you are.

00:27:35.450 --> 00:27:38.310
Laura, dear, I cannot stand these morons any longer.

00:27:39.250 --> 00:27:41.590
If you don't come with me this instant, I shall run amok.

00:27:43.230 --> 00:27:43.990
All right, Waldo.

00:27:46.190 --> 00:27:48.050
10.30 tomorrow, bulletin company.

00:27:48.700 --> 00:27:50.270
You've got a job.

00:27:51.830 --> 00:27:54.910
I concealed my annoyance with masterly self-control.

00:27:56.020 --> 00:27:59.470
But I sensed a situation which would bear watching.

00:28:00.480 --> 00:28:05.450
Laura, take a look at this. Do you like it?

00:28:05.560 --> 00:28:08.270
Do you think it'll make people want to bathe more often?

00:28:08.390 --> 00:28:10.840
It should. It's excellent.

00:28:11.110 --> 00:28:13.570
There you are. Good night, Miss Hunt. Good night.

00:28:13.650 --> 00:28:14.990
- Good night, Mr. Carlton. - Good night, honey.

00:28:15.070 --> 00:28:17.450
It's really very good. Who's the model?

00:28:17.530 --> 00:28:19.030
A girl named Diane Redfern.

00:28:19.110 --> 00:28:21.210
You hired her yourself last week, don't you remember?

00:28:21.290 --> 00:28:23.670
- Oh, yeah. - Well, let's go, moon of my delight.

00:28:23.750 --> 00:28:27.470
- I am starved. - You usually are.

00:28:27.830 --> 00:28:30.000
- I approve of that hat. - You do?

00:28:30.600 --> 00:28:33.420
- There's a good girl in it, too. - Thank you.

00:28:33.420 --> 00:28:39.340
I knew there was something on my mind.

00:28:39.340 --> 00:28:42.240
- What is it? - Yes, will you dine with me tomorrow night?

00:28:42.240 --> 00:28:45.620
- Maybe. - No, that isn't what's worrying me.

00:28:45.620 --> 00:28:47.620
- It's the next night. - But Shelby, I don't think...

00:28:47.620 --> 00:28:50.620
Good. What about three weeks from tonight and all the nights in between?

00:28:50.620 --> 00:28:52.790
Don't you think I have any other engagements?

00:28:52.790 --> 00:28:55.790
What about two months from now and the month after that?

00:28:55.790 --> 00:28:58.030
- What about next year? - That's all settled.

00:28:58.030 --> 00:28:59.410
What about breakfast?

00:29:00.670 --> 00:29:01.470
What about dancing?

00:29:02.100 --> 00:29:03.340
What about lunch?

00:29:03.520 --> 00:29:04.540
Beautiful lunches.

00:29:04.640 --> 00:29:06.300
Day after day after day after day.

00:29:06.300 --> 00:29:07.060
What about work?

00:29:07.490 --> 00:29:08.310
Beautiful work.

00:29:08.680 --> 00:29:10.620
Day after day after day.

00:29:10.660 --> 00:29:14.060
Why, Miss Hunt, the way you talk, you'd think I was in love with you.

00:29:15.750 --> 00:29:20.870
Yes, Laura?

00:29:21.250 --> 00:29:22.500
I heard everything you said.

00:29:24.150 --> 00:29:27.160
I went to call for you.

00:29:27.330 --> 00:29:30.000
I'm sorry.

00:29:31.820 --> 00:29:38.370
And that reminds me, old mother Hubbard has something in the cupboard.

00:29:40.760 --> 00:29:45.720
The results of my private investigation of that sterling character, Mr. Shelby Cobb.

00:29:47.980 --> 00:29:50.550
By stooping so low, you only degrade yourself, Waldo.

00:29:52.110 --> 00:29:54.960
Did you know that he almost went to jail for passing rubber checks?

00:29:55.750 --> 00:29:59.960
That he was suspected of stealing his hostess jewels when he was a house guest of Virginia?

00:30:00.380 --> 00:30:07.210
Naturally, they'd suspect him because he isn't rich. Those are only insinuations, the cheapest kind.

00:30:07.450 --> 00:30:13.060
These aren't only insinuations, my dear. There you are. Read them.

00:30:13.260 --> 00:30:20.460
Whatever. I know his faults. A man can change, can't he?

00:30:20.660 --> 00:30:25.420
People are always ready to hold out a hand to slap you down, but never to pick you up.

00:30:25.620 --> 00:30:30.000
All right, I'm helping, Shelby. His past is his own affair.

00:30:30.990 --> 00:30:32.710
I only care about the present.

00:30:33.910 --> 00:30:37.040
Speaking of the changed Mr. Carpenter in the present tense,

00:30:37.540 --> 00:30:41.440
he's now running around with a model from your own office.

00:30:41.560 --> 00:30:43.730
Her name is Diane Redburn.

00:30:45.220 --> 00:30:49.300
I'm closer to despising you than I thought I ever would be.

00:30:53.360 --> 00:30:59.000
I'm sorry. I should have told you before.

00:31:00.000 --> 00:31:01.920
Delby and I are going to be married next week.

00:31:01.920 --> 00:31:09.060
I believe you presented him with a cigarette case on his last birthday.

00:31:09.060 --> 00:31:15.550
Rather valuable, isn't it?

00:31:15.550 --> 00:31:16.600
Where did you get it?

00:31:16.600 --> 00:31:18.130
From the pawn shop.

00:31:18.130 --> 00:31:20.130
Diane Redfern took it after he gave it to her.

00:31:20.130 --> 00:31:21.660
I don't believe it.

00:31:21.660 --> 00:31:23.840
He probably needed money and was too proud to borrow.

00:31:23.840 --> 00:31:24.840
Carpenter proud?

00:31:24.840 --> 00:31:30.000
Perhaps that's why the pawn ticket was in her name.

00:31:30.670 --> 00:31:32.400
Before this goes any further, I'll just...

00:31:34.930 --> 00:31:46.100
He isn't home. He's dining at Ann Treadwell's.

00:31:46.260 --> 00:31:48.050
He can't be. He asked me to dinner.

00:31:49.070 --> 00:31:51.540
Canceled his appointment with her if you had accepted.

00:31:52.520 --> 00:31:54.320
He treats her rather badly these days.

00:31:56.060 --> 00:31:58.960
I'm afraid she'll say he isn't there.

00:32:08.800 --> 00:32:13.120
Walter, why are you doing this?

00:32:13.940 --> 00:32:24.240
For you, Laura. Shall we pay them a visit?

00:32:25.510 --> 00:32:29.280
He won't be there, Walter. I know he won't.

00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:36.770
Don't you want to take this little bauble along, in case he is there?

00:32:36.770 --> 00:32:50.710
- I'm sorry, Mrs. Treadwell is not home. - We know, dear.

00:33:03.640 --> 00:33:13.960
- Good evening. - I didn't expect to see you tonight.

00:33:14.040 --> 00:33:18.340
There you are, my dear. In a moment it was a supreme disaster. He's trite.

00:33:18.420 --> 00:33:21.400
You've been reading too many melodramas, Waldo.

00:33:22.070 --> 00:33:24.850
I was just telling Ann about our getting married.

00:33:25.200 --> 00:33:30.000
Well, have you two had dinner? Would you like a glass of wine?

00:33:30.000 --> 00:33:42.230
I couldn't find out if she saw carpet in the meantime.

00:33:42.330 --> 00:33:47.660
All I know is that on Friday she had lunch with Diane Redford.

00:33:47.760 --> 00:33:51.020
What came of it, I hoped to hear that night.

00:33:51.120 --> 00:33:55.650
I alternated between moods of over-optimism and over-pessimism.

00:33:55.700 --> 00:33:58.420
When the phone rang, I had a foreboding of disaster.

00:34:03.040 --> 00:34:05.760
I called to tell you, Waldo, I'm frightfully sorry.

00:34:05.760 --> 00:34:07.970
I can't have dinner with you tonight.

00:34:07.970 --> 00:34:09.820
Oh, no, no, I'm not sick.

00:34:09.820 --> 00:34:11.960
I'm just dreadfully nervous.

00:34:11.960 --> 00:34:14.650
I'm going to the country for a few days.

00:34:14.650 --> 00:34:17.690
Yes, I'm afraid it's about Shelby.

00:34:17.690 --> 00:34:19.960
Oh, no, please, there's nothing you can do.

00:34:19.960 --> 00:34:22.580
I've got to think this thing out for myself.

00:34:22.580 --> 00:34:24.220
I'm sorry.

00:34:24.220 --> 00:34:25.970
I'll call you when I get back.

00:34:25.970 --> 00:34:28.130
Bye.

00:34:28.130 --> 00:34:30.000
For the last time I ever heard her

00:34:30.000 --> 00:34:34.740
I was sure she had too much pride to forgive him.

00:34:35.980 --> 00:34:40.880
Where does this Diane Redfern live?

00:34:40.980 --> 00:34:44.070
Brooklyn, somewhere. She's in the phone book.

00:34:44.170 --> 00:34:48.560
Come on, it's late. Let's get going.

00:34:48.660 --> 00:34:51.620
- Waiter. - Yes, sir.

00:34:51.720 --> 00:34:57.560
- All right. - Thank you, Mr. Lydecker.

00:35:07.520 --> 00:35:10.500
I shall never forgive myself for letting her become involved with Shelby.

00:35:10.750 --> 00:35:16.130
It was my fault. I should have stopped it somehow.

00:35:16.360 --> 00:35:23.840
Well, it's too late now. Thanks for the wine.

00:35:43.280 --> 00:35:48.400
Hello, Moscones. This is Lieutenant McPherson, Homicide Bureau.

00:35:48.830 --> 00:35:52.680
Laura Hunt had been buying her liquor from you for several years, hadn't she?

00:35:52.990 --> 00:35:58.480
Yeah. Did she ever buy a brand of scotch called Black Pony?

00:36:00.250 --> 00:36:09.540
- Yeah. I see. That's all I wanted to know, thanks. - She's here, McPherson, the maid.

00:36:09.540 --> 00:36:13.950
Okay. Come in, Miss Clary.

00:36:13.950 --> 00:36:18.790
Never mind the Miss Clary stuff. My name's Bessie and I'm a domestic and I got nothing to be ashamed of.

00:36:18.790 --> 00:36:27.580
- Sit down, Bessie. - Her letters and her private diary.

00:36:27.580 --> 00:36:30.000
You've been reading them, pawing over them.

00:36:30.350 --> 00:36:33.050
It's a shame in the face of the dead. That's what it is. It's a shame.

00:36:33.310 --> 00:36:34.150
Sit down, Bessie.

00:36:34.230 --> 00:36:37.690
I'll stand on my own two feet. Don't you go ordering me around.

00:36:38.270 --> 00:36:41.910
I ain't afraid of cops. I was brought up to spit whenever I saw one.

00:36:43.000 --> 00:36:46.520
Okay. Go ahead and spit. That'll make you feel better.

00:36:52.880 --> 00:36:53.760
What do you want to know?

00:36:54.890 --> 00:36:56.950
What we all want to know. Who killed Laura Hunt.

00:36:57.310 --> 00:36:58.290
How would I know?

00:37:00.730 --> 00:37:01.930
He don't think I done it.

00:37:02.520 --> 00:37:05.560
I know you cops get crazy notions.

00:37:05.880 --> 00:37:08.140
But if he got any notion concerning me,

00:37:08.380 --> 00:37:11.470
ask anyone, anyone who ever come to this house,

00:37:12.070 --> 00:37:13.600
I would have worked for her.

00:37:13.760 --> 00:37:17.510
I would have washed, ironed, scrubbed, done everything she wanted of me,

00:37:17.710 --> 00:37:19.300
whether she paid me for it or not.

00:37:19.850 --> 00:37:23.650
And it wasn't only on account of the thousand sweet things she'd done for me.

00:37:23.790 --> 00:37:26.080
It was because she was so sweet herself.

00:37:26.500 --> 00:37:29.540
Because she was a real fine lady.

00:37:31.120 --> 00:37:35.960
- But you cops wouldn't know about that. - But you do.

00:37:36.860 --> 00:37:39.320
That's all the more reason why you should help me, Bessie.

00:37:40.440 --> 00:37:42.990
Do you happen to know how this got into her liquor cabinet?

00:37:45.040 --> 00:37:48.840
- I put it there. - But she never bought cheap stuff like that.

00:37:49.000 --> 00:37:51.160
- Not a lady like Miss Hunt. - No.

00:37:51.860 --> 00:37:54.450
- When did you put it in the cabinet? - Saturday.

00:37:54.610 --> 00:37:57.570
Before the police came. Was it there Friday night before you left?

00:37:57.980 --> 00:38:00.000
- No. - Are you sure of that?

00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:05.220
I cleaned out the cabinet on Friday and put the empties in the basement.

00:38:05.320 --> 00:38:10.740
Then somebody was with her in the apartment Friday night, someone who brought that bottle.

00:38:10.840 --> 00:38:12.460
- Yes. - Who?

00:38:12.560 --> 00:38:17.710
I don't know, but I didn't want anyone getting any wrong ideas about her.

00:38:17.810 --> 00:38:24.270
God rest her soul. That's why I took it out of the bedroom and put it in the cabinet before the police got here.

00:38:24.370 --> 00:38:30.000
And that ain't all I done. I washed out the glasses and cleaned off the bottle today.

00:38:30.000 --> 00:38:33.800
- Do you know what happens to people who destroy evidence? - I don't care.

00:38:33.940 --> 00:38:38.470
You ain't gonna tell the newspaper reporters, are you?

00:38:38.580 --> 00:38:41.890
And let them make up their nasty stories and drag her name through the mud?

00:38:42.070 --> 00:38:47.210
Go ahead, but it won't do you any good. I'll say you lied.

00:38:47.410 --> 00:38:49.210
- I'll say you come here... - Take it easy, Bessie.

00:38:49.360 --> 00:38:53.300
- Give me some ice and a set-up, will you? - All right.

00:38:54.900 --> 00:38:56.600
A couple of highball glasses.

00:39:02.080 --> 00:39:03.960
- Good morning. - Good morning, Lieutenant McPherson.

00:39:03.960 --> 00:39:05.800
- Good morning, Lieutenant. - Hello, McPherson.

00:39:05.800 --> 00:39:08.320
This is quite a delegation. I only sent for you, Collin.

00:39:08.320 --> 00:39:10.480
Well, Shelby's dropping me off at the hairdressers later,

00:39:10.480 --> 00:39:12.240
so I thought I might as well come along.

00:39:12.240 --> 00:39:14.260
My excuse is equally feeble.

00:39:14.260 --> 00:39:16.420
I just popped in to pay my dubious respects

00:39:16.420 --> 00:39:19.020
and inquires to the state of your health.

00:39:19.020 --> 00:39:19.970
Insipid, I trust.

00:39:19.970 --> 00:39:22.130
I was just going to pour myself a drink. Would you care to join me?

00:39:22.130 --> 00:39:24.850
Well, that's a very nice idea, Shelby. Wouldn't you like one?

00:39:24.850 --> 00:39:26.340
- I'll get it. - Never mind.

00:39:26.340 --> 00:39:28.580
- Now, Bessie, would you bring a couple more glasses, please? - Yes, sir.

00:39:28.580 --> 00:39:29.600
Hi, Bessie.

00:39:30.000 --> 00:39:35.060
- What are you doing here? I'm paid up for the week and I'm working regardless. - Would you like one, Lidegger?

00:39:35.060 --> 00:39:39.350
I see no reason to exclude myself. The host provides scotch. - Does it do?

00:39:39.350 --> 00:39:43.330
- I presume it'll have to. - How about you, Carpenter? It's cheap, but it's potent.

00:39:43.330 --> 00:39:46.930
As a matter of fact, I don't think I care for any. I'm not much of a daytime drinker.

00:39:46.930 --> 00:39:53.300
- That'll be all for you, Bessie. You can go home now. - But I... Yes, sir.

00:39:53.300 --> 00:39:57.700
I remember when Laura bought these glasses. She loved them. She loved all her things, sir.

00:39:57.700 --> 00:39:59.820
- What are you going to do? - Sell them.

00:40:00.320 --> 00:40:02.680
I don't know. I suppose so. Thank you.

00:40:03.040 --> 00:40:06.860
If I'm appointed administrator of the estate, I shall probably just call him Corey.

00:40:07.180 --> 00:40:11.440
- You mean Lancaster Corey, the art dealer? - Yes, he was a friend of Laura's.

00:40:11.490 --> 00:40:14.400
Let him dispose of everything. It'll be less gruesome that way.

00:40:14.790 --> 00:40:16.270
Not quite everything, Anne.

00:40:17.670 --> 00:40:19.970
Two or three things in here belong to me.

00:40:20.290 --> 00:40:22.370
This spas, for instance.

00:40:22.960 --> 00:40:28.100
And that clock, of course, and the antique fire screen.

00:40:28.310 --> 00:40:30.000
- I will lend them to Laura, you know. - Oh!

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:31.990
- Oh, really, Waldo. - Yes, really.

00:40:32.170 --> 00:40:35.380
This vase is the gem of my collection. I intend to have it back.

00:40:35.420 --> 00:40:37.360
- And the clock on the screen, too. - But they aren't yours.

00:40:37.440 --> 00:40:39.190
You gave them to Lala. I won't permit it.

00:40:39.760 --> 00:40:42.360
Doesn't alleged fiancé have any voice in this matter?

00:40:43.160 --> 00:40:47.640
I'll take the vase with me now and send someone to collect the other things this very day.

00:40:48.680 --> 00:40:51.410
Nothing's leaving here except you, Lydica.

00:40:51.480 --> 00:40:54.680
Is that your quaint way of indicating dismissal?

00:40:55.180 --> 00:40:58.660
We're all going anyway. I have to be back at headquarters by noon.

00:40:58.740 --> 00:41:00.000
But, Lieutenant, I don't understand you.

00:41:00.000 --> 00:41:03.580
- You said you sent for me, didn't you? - Yeah.

00:41:03.580 --> 00:41:06.340
Well, don't you want to see me? Don't you want to ask me some questions?

00:41:06.340 --> 00:41:08.010
- I'll be seeing you. - Well...

00:41:08.010 --> 00:41:10.740
- I bid you good day. - Come along, Shelby.

00:41:10.740 --> 00:41:13.240
Are you making any progress on the case, Lieutenant?

00:41:13.240 --> 00:41:16.200
We're doing all right.

00:41:33.040 --> 00:41:34.080
- Oh, huh?

00:41:34.080 --> 00:41:35.100
- I've got something to eat.

00:41:35.100 --> 00:41:35.940
I'll take over.

00:41:35.940 --> 00:41:36.860
- Thanks.

00:41:36.860 --> 00:41:38.930
- Take your time.

00:42:36.270 --> 00:42:36.880
Thank you.

00:44:47.120 --> 00:44:51.090
- Did Mac Fisson, any calls come through?

00:44:51.090 --> 00:44:53.000
- Well, keep listening.

00:44:53.000 --> 00:44:54.420
- No, nothing new.

00:45:01.340 --> 00:45:02.580
I happen to see the lights on.

00:45:04.870 --> 00:45:05.990
Have you subleth's apartment?

00:45:06.820 --> 00:45:08.260
You're here often enough to pay rent.

00:45:08.820 --> 00:45:09.440
Any objections?

00:45:10.460 --> 00:45:10.900
Yes.

00:45:12.120 --> 00:45:13.980
I object to you prying into Laura's letters.

00:45:14.450 --> 00:45:15.590
Especially those from me.

00:45:16.230 --> 00:45:16.550
Why?

00:45:16.830 --> 00:45:17.970
Yours are the best in the bunch.

00:45:18.090 --> 00:45:18.430
Thanks.

00:45:19.060 --> 00:45:20.670
But I didn't write them to you.

00:45:22.060 --> 00:45:23.360
Haven't you any sense of privacy?

00:45:24.710 --> 00:45:27.520
Murder victims have no claim to privacy.

00:45:28.640 --> 00:45:30.000
Have detectives who buy portraits of murderers

00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:36.300
murder victims a claim to privacy? Lancaster Corey told me that you've already put in a bid

00:45:36.300 --> 00:45:42.220
for it. That's none of your business. McPherson, did it ever strike you that you're acting very

00:45:42.220 --> 00:45:46.890
strangely? It's a wonder you don't come here like a suitor with roses and a box of candy.

00:45:47.800 --> 00:45:54.800
Drugstore candy, of course. Have you ever dreamed of Laura as your wife by your side at the policeman's

00:45:54.800 --> 00:46:00.000
ball or in the bleachers or listening to the heroic story of how you got a silver shin bone from

00:46:00.000 --> 00:46:00.960
a gun battle with the gangster?

00:46:02.770 --> 00:46:04.460
I see you have.

00:46:04.500 --> 00:46:05.340
Why don't you go home?

00:46:05.400 --> 00:46:05.880
I'm busy.

00:46:08.300 --> 00:46:10.070
Perhaps we can come to terms now.

00:46:11.850 --> 00:46:12.550
You want the portrait?

00:46:13.220 --> 00:46:14.020
Perfectly understandable.

00:46:14.880 --> 00:46:16.040
I want my possessions,

00:46:16.400 --> 00:46:16.900
my bars,

00:46:17.020 --> 00:46:17.540
my clock,

00:46:17.580 --> 00:46:18.180
and my screen.

00:46:18.650 --> 00:46:19.730
Also perfectly understandable.

00:46:20.710 --> 00:46:21.170
Now, if you...

00:46:21.170 --> 00:46:21.730
Get going.

00:46:23.070 --> 00:46:23.870
You better watch out,

00:46:23.950 --> 00:46:24.330
McPherson,

00:46:24.430 --> 00:46:25.030
or you'll end up

00:46:25.030 --> 00:46:26.110
at a psychiatric ward.

00:46:27.190 --> 00:46:28.540
I don't think they've ever

00:46:28.540 --> 00:46:29.130
had a patient

00:46:29.130 --> 00:46:30.000
who fell in love

00:46:30.000 --> 00:46:31.250
with a corpse?

00:48:02.240 --> 00:48:03.080
What are you doing here?

00:48:05.740 --> 00:48:11.360
If you don't get out at once, I'm going to call the police.

00:48:11.900 --> 00:48:13.710
You are Laura Hunt, aren't you?

00:48:14.840 --> 00:48:17.660
Aren't you?

00:48:18.110 --> 00:48:19.510
I'm going to call the police.

00:48:19.880 --> 00:48:20.960
Well, I am the police.

00:48:22.420 --> 00:48:23.160
Mark McPherson.

00:48:24.150 --> 00:48:26.880
What's this all about?

00:48:27.940 --> 00:48:29.490
Do you know?

00:48:30.480 --> 00:48:34.120
- Don't you know what's happened?

00:48:34.120 --> 00:48:36.000
Haven't you seen the papers?

00:48:36.000 --> 00:48:37.880
Where have you been?

00:48:37.880 --> 00:48:41.750
- Up in the country, I don't get a newspaper.

00:48:41.750 --> 00:48:44.100
- Haven't you got a radio?

00:48:44.100 --> 00:48:45.080
- It was broken.

00:49:05.340 --> 00:49:08.150
Somebody was murdered in this room.

00:49:08.150 --> 00:49:12.070
- Do you have any idea who it was? - No.

00:49:12.070 --> 00:49:16.690
- Who had a key to your apartment? - Nobody.

00:49:16.690 --> 00:49:21.230
- You sure? - When did it happen?

00:49:21.230 --> 00:49:28.550
- Friday night. - What are you gonna do now?

00:49:28.550 --> 00:49:29.830
Find out who was murdered.

00:49:30.920 --> 00:49:38.000
and then find the murderer to take off those wet clothes.

00:49:38.470 --> 00:49:39.350
You might catch cold.

00:49:46.190 --> 00:49:57.000
Found this in my closet.

00:49:57.880 --> 00:49:59.220
It's Diane Redford's.

00:50:00.000 --> 00:50:04.550
I wasn't here when I left. She's one of our models, just about my size.

00:50:05.110 --> 00:50:10.370
- Beautiful, wasn't she? - Do you suppose?

00:50:10.840 --> 00:50:23.940
Sit down, please. This is Monday night. You left on Friday.

00:50:24.160 --> 00:50:27.460
- Rather a long weekend, isn't it? - Yes.

00:50:27.810 --> 00:50:29.280
What train did you take?

00:50:30.440 --> 00:50:32.610
See anybody you know on the train?

00:50:32.610 --> 00:50:33.610
No.

00:50:33.610 --> 00:50:34.840
Then what?

00:50:34.840 --> 00:50:37.780
Then I got off the train at Norwalk.

00:50:37.780 --> 00:50:40.660
So nobody you knew at the station either?

00:50:40.660 --> 00:50:41.640
No.

00:50:41.640 --> 00:50:43.560
Go on.

00:50:43.560 --> 00:50:46.120
Then I went to the garage where I keep my car.

00:50:46.120 --> 00:50:48.490
It's a private garage.

00:50:48.490 --> 00:50:50.920
Nobody saw me there either.

00:50:50.920 --> 00:50:52.520
Then I drove to my house.

00:50:52.520 --> 00:50:55.110
You were there three days.

00:50:55.110 --> 00:50:56.110
What did you do?

00:50:56.110 --> 00:50:57.250
Worked in my garden.

00:50:57.250 --> 00:51:00.000
You didn't go out there?

00:51:00.000 --> 00:51:03.600
No. I had everything I needed in the house.

00:51:03.600 --> 00:51:06.910
- Nobody came to see you? - Nobody.

00:51:06.910 --> 00:51:08.520
I went there to be alone.

00:51:08.520 --> 00:51:12.180
Police were there on Saturday. There was no one in the house.

00:51:12.180 --> 00:51:16.300
Oh, yes. Saturday, I took a long walk.

00:51:16.300 --> 00:51:18.820
I walked for hours in the woods.

00:51:18.820 --> 00:51:20.410
Mm-hmm.

00:51:20.410 --> 00:51:24.000
You were going to marry Shelby Carpenter this week.

00:51:24.000 --> 00:51:29.890
- Thursday, if I'm not mistaken. - Yes.

00:51:31.050 --> 00:51:35.670
Yet you went away just before your wedding for a long weekend, to be alone.

00:51:35.730 --> 00:51:38.710
I was tired. I'd been working hard.

00:51:38.750 --> 00:51:42.420
You know Shelby Carpenter has a key to this apartment. Why didn't you tell me?

00:51:42.420 --> 00:51:44.790
I know nothing of the sort. He hasn't.

00:51:44.870 --> 00:51:46.870
How else did the girl get into the apartment?

00:51:46.900 --> 00:51:50.480
You knew she was in love with Carpenter, that he'd given her your cigarette case.

00:51:50.490 --> 00:51:51.760
You know all that, don't you?

00:51:52.060 --> 00:51:55.020
I knew that she was in love with him. She told me so herself.

00:51:55.100 --> 00:51:57.650
- When did she tell you? - At lunch last Friday.

00:51:57.950 --> 00:52:00.000
I also know that she meant nothing to Shelby.

00:52:00.830 --> 00:52:04.450
- I understand him better than you do. - She was found in your dressing gown and slippers.

00:52:04.490 --> 00:52:09.350
That's hardly the regulation costume for an impersonal chat between a man and woman who mean nothing to each other.

00:52:09.470 --> 00:52:12.910
Did you know or did you suspect that he was going to bring her here Friday night, Miss Hunt?

00:52:12.990 --> 00:52:15.680
How could I? I don't know that he brought her here. Neither do you.

00:52:15.680 --> 00:52:18.210
- You merely assume it. - What other assumption is possible?

00:52:18.230 --> 00:52:21.440
Do you love this fellow carpenter so much you risk your own safety to protect him?

00:52:21.500 --> 00:52:24.040
My own safety? Do you suspect me?

00:52:24.230 --> 00:52:27.720
I suspect nobody and everybody. I'm merely trying to get at the truth.

00:52:28.110 --> 00:52:30.000
I see you have been trying to get at the truth.

00:52:30.570 --> 00:52:32.950
You've read things I never met anyone else to look at.

00:52:33.780 --> 00:52:37.110
Strictly routine. I'm sorry, really.

00:52:40.800 --> 00:52:42.900
I'd better be going. I'll see you in the morning, Miss Hunt.

00:52:43.670 --> 00:52:46.770
Oh, I must ask you not to leave the house or use the phone.

00:52:48.220 --> 00:52:50.440
But I've got to let my friends know I'm alive.

00:52:50.640 --> 00:52:53.200
Sorry, Miss Hunt, but I must insist you do as I say.

00:52:53.330 --> 00:52:56.330
- Am I under arrest? - No.

00:52:56.350 --> 00:52:59.800
If anything should happen to you this time, I wouldn't like it.

00:53:01.440 --> 00:53:03.150
- All right, I promise.

00:53:03.150 --> 00:53:04.770
- There's one more thing.

00:53:04.770 --> 00:53:06.350
You may as well know what I know.

00:53:06.350 --> 00:53:08.010
Some of it at any rate.

00:53:08.010 --> 00:53:11.690
It'll save time and a lot of unnecessary fencing.

00:53:11.690 --> 00:53:17.410
I know that you went away to make up your mind

00:53:17.410 --> 00:53:22.140
whether you would marry Shelby Carpenter or not.

00:53:22.140 --> 00:53:23.900
What did you decide?

00:53:23.900 --> 00:53:27.050
I want the truth.

00:53:27.050 --> 00:53:28.470
- I decided not to marry him.

00:53:30.570 --> 00:53:31.750
- I'll see you in the morning.

00:53:31.750 --> 00:53:33.120
Good night.

00:53:33.120 --> 00:53:50.650
- Good night.

00:53:50.650 --> 00:53:53.750
- The person know that?

00:53:53.750 --> 00:53:56.590
Okay, bye.

00:53:56.590 --> 00:53:59.320
- Say, Mark, that was the medical examiner's report.

00:53:59.320 --> 00:54:00.000
It wasn't Laurel.

00:54:00.440 --> 00:54:02.700
It was Diane Redfern that was bumped off upstairs.

00:54:02.700 --> 00:54:05.330
- Kind of balls things up, doesn't it? - Yeah.

00:54:05.330 --> 00:54:07.170
Say, you're not taking it too hard.

00:54:07.170 --> 00:54:18.370
- Put you in my car in front of bullets. - What is that?

00:54:18.370 --> 00:54:22.530
Yeah. Dames are always pulling a switch on you.

00:54:22.530 --> 00:54:23.680
Stand by.

00:54:42.160 --> 00:54:43.570
I'm gonna tail him.

00:55:36.300 --> 00:55:38.220
Are you taking it down or putting it away, Carpenter?

00:55:38.550 --> 00:55:52.960
You've been fired lately.

00:55:53.260 --> 00:55:54.700
Yes, I killed some rabbits with it.

00:55:54.900 --> 00:55:55.220
When?

00:55:55.420 --> 00:55:57.180
Oh, a while back. I don't know exactly.

00:55:57.580 --> 00:55:59.150
You know about guns, don't you?

00:55:59.600 --> 00:56:00.000
Yes.

00:56:01.050 --> 00:56:04.650
- How come you didn't clean it afterward? - I don't know. I forgot, I suppose.

00:56:04.980 --> 00:56:09.030
- Your initials? - Yes, I gave it to Laura for protection.

00:56:09.130 --> 00:56:13.760
She didn't want it, but I insisted. This house is rather isolated, as you see.

00:56:14.240 --> 00:56:16.160
- Did you teach her how to use it? - No.

00:56:16.890 --> 00:56:19.810
- Does she know how? - I don't know. It didn't occur to me to ask.

00:56:20.680 --> 00:56:26.290
- You're a vague sort of a fellow, aren't you, Carpenter? - I've spent very little time in observing my own character, Mr. McPherson.

00:56:26.490 --> 00:56:30.000
- You haven't borrowed it lately. You didn't just bring it back tonight. - You followed me here.

00:56:30.000 --> 00:56:32.000
You saw me come in. You ought to know.

00:56:32.000 --> 00:56:35.920
You realize the spot you're in, Carpenter?

00:56:35.920 --> 00:56:38.740
You took that poor girl to Miss Hunt's apartment.

00:56:38.740 --> 00:56:40.740
You knew all along it was she who was murdered.

00:56:40.740 --> 00:56:46.740
Didn't you know Laura Hunt would come back any day and spill the whole thing?

00:56:46.740 --> 00:56:50.610
Or did you plan to kill her, too?

00:56:50.610 --> 00:56:53.660
Hide the body someplace and cover up your first crime?

00:56:53.660 --> 00:56:55.600
You're being fantastic, McPherson.

00:56:55.600 --> 00:56:59.850
You took a bottle of Black Pony to her house Friday night.

00:57:00.190 --> 00:57:04.180
- I took it there over a week ago. - Bessie said it wasn't there Friday night.

00:57:04.260 --> 00:57:07.780
- It was Saturday morning. - I can't help what Bessie said.

00:57:10.360 --> 00:57:13.160
- Where's the key to her apartment? - I haven't got it.

00:57:13.200 --> 00:57:18.120
- What did you do with it? Give it back to her tonight? - I never had one.

00:57:18.240 --> 00:57:20.910
Okay, you didn't take the bottle of Scots to her house Friday night

00:57:20.990 --> 00:57:23.330
and you haven't got a key to her apartment. How did you get in?

00:57:23.410 --> 00:57:24.910
- Well, I... - You had a key and I know it.

00:57:24.990 --> 00:57:30.000
- Now, come on, Carpenter, spill it. - I... Laura kept a duplicate.

00:57:30.030 --> 00:57:35.010
at her office. I went over and got it. I'd asked Diane to dine with me. I wanted to have it out with

00:57:35.010 --> 00:57:41.490
her once and for all. You know, she thought, well, she thought she was in love with me. Well,

00:57:41.490 --> 00:57:45.810
we couldn't go on talking in public places. She was too upset. I couldn't go to her room and I

00:57:45.810 --> 00:57:51.030
didn't want to take her to my hotel. So we went to Laura's. Yeah. We talked there for about three

00:57:51.030 --> 00:57:58.230
hours. Then the doorbell rang and go on. Diane was frightened, but knowing Laura's I've known her,

00:57:58.230 --> 00:57:59.920
I've learned to be surprised at nothing.

00:58:00.700 --> 00:58:01.850
What do you mean by that?

00:58:01.950 --> 00:58:05.960
Well, her friends would come to her with her troubles at all hours of the day or night.

00:58:06.110 --> 00:58:08.540
I asked Diane to answer the door.

00:58:08.620 --> 00:58:12.390
- Why didn't you go yourself? - Well, supposing one of Laura's friends had found me there.

00:58:12.480 --> 00:58:15.180
- Why open the door at all? - They must have seen the light.

00:58:15.450 --> 00:58:18.210
What about the girl, Diane? What about her reputation?

00:58:18.290 --> 00:58:22.030
Well, I asked her to tell them that Laura had lent her the apartment while she was away.

00:58:22.110 --> 00:58:24.300
Anybody that knew Laura would have believed that.

00:58:24.390 --> 00:58:27.730
- Yeah, go on. - Well, then the doorbell rang again.

00:58:27.880 --> 00:58:29.510
I could hear Diane's mules.

00:58:30.000 --> 00:58:32.920
Laura's they were, clattering across the bare boards between the rugs.

00:58:33.770 --> 00:58:35.890
Then there was a moment's silence and then a shot.

00:58:36.800 --> 00:58:38.650
It was an awful explosion.

00:58:40.380 --> 00:58:42.640
By the time I reached her, the door was closed.

00:58:43.410 --> 00:58:44.550
She lay there on the floor.

00:58:45.080 --> 00:58:46.100
You got to see who it was?

00:58:46.160 --> 00:58:49.260
No, I was too confused, too horrified, incapable of doing anything.

00:58:50.030 --> 00:58:50.790
The room was dark.

00:58:50.850 --> 00:58:52.970
I saw only a vague heap lying on the floor.

00:58:53.360 --> 00:58:56.240
I don't think I fully grasped the situation.

00:58:57.360 --> 00:58:59.220
I think I called her name, but I'm not sure.

00:58:59.220 --> 00:58:59.600
I...

00:59:00.540 --> 00:59:03.420
I remember her kneeling on the floor, feeling her heart.

00:59:05.140 --> 00:59:08.050
- My first instinct was to call the police. - Why didn't you?

00:59:08.110 --> 00:59:09.280
I don't know.

00:59:09.350 --> 00:59:12.530
Rather, I was afraid, not only for myself, but for Laura.

00:59:12.750 --> 00:59:16.580
In a panicky sort of way, I felt that I must keep out of this, to keep Laura out of it.

00:59:17.250 --> 00:59:21.880
Oh, I know now how foolish and hopeless it was, but there was only one thing on my mind.

00:59:22.110 --> 00:59:26.270
The safety of a person whose life was dearer to me than my own. Don't you understand that?

00:59:26.780 --> 00:59:29.920
Did you think Laura had done it? Did you?

00:59:30.600 --> 00:59:31.800
I don't remember what I thought.

00:59:32.640 --> 00:59:33.340
Do you think so now?

00:59:34.450 --> 00:59:34.770
No.

00:59:35.330 --> 00:59:35.940
But you didn't.

00:59:36.040 --> 00:59:36.300
No.

00:59:37.640 --> 00:59:41.000
On Saturday, when our men went to the hotel to tell you that Laura was dead,

00:59:41.040 --> 00:59:42.360
you seemed sincerely shocked.

00:59:42.500 --> 00:59:43.060
I was.

00:59:43.160 --> 00:59:44.760
I hadn't expected that mistake.

00:59:44.980 --> 00:59:47.000
But you had your alibi ready, no matter who was dead.

00:59:47.260 --> 00:59:49.420
Yet you knew the minute Laura got back, it wouldn't stick.

00:59:49.980 --> 00:59:50.860
Don't you see?

00:59:50.940 --> 00:59:52.900
I was incapable of thinking that far ahead.

00:59:52.960 --> 00:59:54.760
I was incapable of thinking at all.

00:59:55.280 --> 00:59:58.390
I was groping for some way to keep Laura's name out of it.

00:59:58.860 --> 01:00:00.000
I was heartbroken.

01:00:00.000 --> 01:00:03.170
about Diane, and panic-stricken about Laura.

01:00:03.320 --> 01:00:05.990
I haven't slept a full two hours since this thing happened.

01:00:06.070 --> 01:00:07.520
Let's get back to the present.

01:00:07.580 --> 01:00:09.350
What did you and Laura talk about tonight?

01:00:09.400 --> 01:00:12.920
I told her the whole story, just as I told you.

01:00:13.900 --> 01:00:16.060
She phoned you after she promised me she wouldn't call anybody.

01:00:16.140 --> 01:00:17.200
What did she want?

01:00:17.280 --> 01:00:19.200
It's perfectly natural she should want to see me,

01:00:19.280 --> 01:00:21.280
especially after what's happened.

01:00:21.330 --> 01:00:22.590
Why don't you tell the truth, Calviter?

01:00:22.670 --> 01:00:23.970
She sent her here to get rid of this gun.

01:00:24.050 --> 01:00:25.630
She did not. It was my own idea.

01:00:25.710 --> 01:00:27.400
She doesn't even know I'm here.

01:00:34.160 --> 01:00:37.110
- It works fine, doesn't it? - Yes, did you think it wouldn't?

01:00:37.110 --> 01:00:41.430
I hoped it wouldn't. All right, let's go.

01:00:41.430 --> 01:00:46.640
- Am I under arrest? - No, but you're not to leave town.

01:00:46.720 --> 01:00:48.240
It would be foolish of you to try it.

01:01:08.410 --> 01:01:13.750
- Good morning. - Good morning.

01:01:13.750 --> 01:01:23.280
Breakfast. You didn't buy any food when you went out last night.

01:01:23.280 --> 01:01:27.710
I'll pick some bacon and eggs. Can you make coffee?

01:01:27.710 --> 01:01:30.000
Suppose you set the table while I get breakfast.

01:01:32.110 --> 01:01:35.500
- Do you always sound like this in the morning? - Don't tell me. You can cook.

01:01:35.810 --> 01:01:39.750
My mother always listened sympathetically to my dreams of a career.

01:01:40.020 --> 01:01:44.760
- Then taught me another recipe. - Don't move.

01:01:48.330 --> 01:01:59.910
It's all been a mistake, Bessie. I'm not a ghost, really.

01:02:00.060 --> 01:02:03.140
Luda, me and you did.

01:02:03.900 --> 01:02:09.770
It was Miss Redfern's body you found taking over here,

01:02:10.830 --> 01:02:12.410
fixing us some coffee and eggs.

01:02:13.120 --> 01:02:17.050
But I saw it with my own eyes.

01:02:18.660 --> 01:02:19.700
I don't understand.

01:02:20.040 --> 01:02:22.020
Have you ever heard a ghost ask for eggs?

01:02:23.200 --> 01:02:23.960
No, Miss.

01:02:25.070 --> 01:02:26.050
You'll be all right, Miss.

01:02:37.360 --> 01:02:40.140
Somebody should have warned her. Poor Bessie.

01:02:40.440 --> 01:02:43.060
By the way, I've asked Waldo Lidecker to come here this morning.

01:02:43.470 --> 01:02:44.300
Did you tell him?

01:02:44.910 --> 01:02:45.250
No.

01:02:46.060 --> 01:02:47.280
Why not? It's brutal.

01:02:47.950 --> 01:02:49.170
I'm not doing it for fun.

01:02:51.680 --> 01:02:54.300
Why did you break your promise and go out and see Shelby last night?

01:02:54.800 --> 01:02:56.500
You forced me to give you my word.

01:02:57.270 --> 01:03:00.000
I never have been and I never will be bound by anything I do.

01:03:00.000 --> 01:03:02.310
that I don't do of my own free will.

01:03:02.310 --> 01:03:05.380
Redfern was in love with Carpenter.

01:03:05.380 --> 01:03:06.380
He admitted that last night.

01:03:06.380 --> 01:03:08.770
May I have a match, please?

01:03:08.770 --> 01:03:09.730
Oh, I'm sorry.

01:03:09.730 --> 01:03:11.850
I also told you that he wasn't in love with her.

01:03:11.850 --> 01:03:23.800
Just sit still.

01:03:23.800 --> 01:03:25.760
Hello, Lieutenant.

01:03:25.760 --> 01:03:27.480
Morning, my darling.

01:03:27.480 --> 01:03:28.640
Hello, dear.

01:03:31.210 --> 01:03:34.220
- Thank you. - Oh, it's on again.

01:03:34.300 --> 01:03:38.160
Do I have to get a permit from the police department to kiss my fiancee good morning?

01:03:38.250 --> 01:03:40.160
So he made you change your mind?

01:03:40.240 --> 01:03:44.620
Speaking of changing one's mind, Mr. McPherson, I have just come from my lawyer.

01:03:44.700 --> 01:03:47.430
Yeah? Did he tell you how much you get off for good behavior?

01:03:47.510 --> 01:03:51.050
No, but he told me that anything I may have said last night was said under duress

01:03:51.130 --> 01:03:54.760
and can't be used against me. Besides, none of it was true.

01:03:54.860 --> 01:03:56.970
Smart lawyer you've got, Carpenter.

01:03:57.050 --> 01:04:00.000
Maybe he told you how that scotch got up here Friday night after you'd bought it at Mr. McPherson.

01:04:00.000 --> 01:04:03.420
Maybe it was the lawyer who brought Diane Redfern up here.

01:04:03.420 --> 01:04:14.470
Well, McPherson, have you thought over the deal I suggested?

01:04:31.340 --> 01:04:32.950
- Right. - And pocket.

01:04:33.820 --> 01:04:35.840
We'd better take him into the bedroom.

01:04:35.920 --> 01:04:50.740
- What? - You can't say anything, dear. Just be quiet.

01:04:53.730 --> 01:04:56.910
You know, I think this is carrying things too far.

01:04:56.990 --> 01:04:59.370
Your methods are vicious. There ought to be a law against it.

01:04:59.450 --> 01:05:00.000
Yeah?

01:05:00.570 --> 01:05:04.570
- How does he feel now? - It was a terrible shock, poor darling.

01:05:04.650 --> 01:05:08.520
- Don't tell me you're in love with him too. - Look here, fella, you're not to talk that way to Ms. Hunt.

01:05:08.530 --> 01:05:11.800
- Shut up. Why do you cover up for a guy like him? - Don't answer him, Lara.

01:05:11.880 --> 01:05:15.060
- What story did he tell you last night? - Don't answer him. Let him talk to our lawyer.

01:05:15.140 --> 01:05:19.500
- Our lawyer? So now you're covering up for each other. - Look at him, Lara. He's beginning to crack up.

01:05:19.580 --> 01:05:23.160
He'd use anything to make an arrest just so he can be a big shot in the headlines.

01:05:23.240 --> 01:05:27.200
- I've got enough on you to arrest you right now. - Quick, my person in the handcuffs.

01:05:27.280 --> 01:05:30.000
- Trundle him off to the who's gal. - You keep out of this.

01:05:30.000 --> 01:05:31.260
- You look nice in bracelets, Carpenter.

01:05:31.260 --> 01:05:33.300
- Why don't you get down on all fours again, Waldo?

01:05:33.300 --> 01:05:35.920
It's the only time you've ever kept your mouth shut.

01:05:35.920 --> 01:05:39.050
- Oh, we'll forgive my wee touch of epilepsy, my dear.

01:05:39.050 --> 01:05:41.350
It's an old family custom.

01:05:41.350 --> 01:05:44.700
Well, McPherson, what does Laura's resurrection do to you?

01:05:44.700 --> 01:05:47.750
- Too bad Diane Redfern can't be resurrected.

01:05:47.750 --> 01:05:50.850
- I'm afraid I've interrupted what you call a pinch.

01:05:50.850 --> 01:05:53.360
Do your duty, McPherson.

01:05:53.360 --> 01:05:56.660
- You know, Lidecker, you've made me change my mind.

01:05:56.660 --> 01:05:57.940
For the moment.

01:05:57.940 --> 01:05:59.250
- Well, in that case, we'll have time

01:05:59.250 --> 01:06:00.000
for a little get-together.

01:06:00.890 --> 01:06:03.380
- You better order some liquor and some food, Laura.

01:06:03.380 --> 01:06:05.140
- What for?

01:06:05.140 --> 01:06:07.100
- People are coming to celebrate your return.

01:06:07.100 --> 01:06:09.320
Ann and Bullitt and Corey, everybody.

01:06:09.320 --> 01:06:10.440
- Who asked them to come?

01:06:10.440 --> 01:06:12.700
- I did, when I wasn't there.

01:06:12.700 --> 01:06:14.610
I phoned my man and he's calling everyone.

01:06:14.610 --> 01:06:16.520
- Why did you do that, Waldo?

01:06:16.520 --> 01:06:18.780
- A sense of the fitness of things, my dear.

01:06:18.780 --> 01:06:20.660
Perhaps our friend can weave all the loose ends

01:06:20.660 --> 01:06:21.710
into a noose.

01:06:21.710 --> 01:06:22.550
Amy Pherson?

01:06:22.550 --> 01:06:23.530
- Sorry, you went to all that trouble.

01:06:23.530 --> 01:06:30.000
I've already called them.

01:06:30.000 --> 01:06:32.000
I haven't had a moment with you all day.

01:06:32.000 --> 01:06:34.000
It's a very thirsty crowd, you know.

01:06:34.000 --> 01:06:36.000
After all, it isn't your homecoming.

01:06:36.000 --> 01:06:38.000
Aren't you being a little bit nasty, Anne?

01:06:38.000 --> 01:06:40.460
I feel nasty when I don't see you.

01:06:40.460 --> 01:06:44.460
Well, you look very sweet. That's a completely wonderful hat, darling.

01:06:44.460 --> 01:06:46.700
Now, if you'll forgive me, Laura wants a cocktail.

01:06:46.700 --> 01:06:48.780
So do I.

01:06:48.780 --> 01:06:50.700
Well, here you go.

01:06:50.700 --> 01:06:53.780
Shelby, why don't you come to your senses?

01:06:53.780 --> 01:06:55.670
You know it's all over between you and Laura.

01:06:55.670 --> 01:06:57.670
Well, it soon will be.

01:06:57.670 --> 01:07:00.000
You haven't lost me.

01:07:00.570 --> 01:07:04.780
- Why don't we get married? - But, Anne, dear, you don't seem to realize the situation.

01:07:05.020 --> 01:07:08.060
That's why you need me. We'll get the best lawyer that money can buy.

01:07:08.480 --> 01:07:12.100
And when it's all over, we'll go away, any way you want to, and forget about all this.

01:07:12.300 --> 01:07:15.180
Thanks, Anne, but you see, Laura needs me. Sorry.

01:07:20.300 --> 01:07:27.240
- Thanks. - If you don't mind, I'd like a word with Miss Hunt.

01:07:27.680 --> 01:07:30.000
I don't mind? Talk to her as much as you like.

01:07:31.660 --> 01:07:34.120
See, he's taking a new tack.

01:07:34.120 --> 01:07:35.010
What do you mean?

01:07:35.010 --> 01:07:38.340
Trying to make you like him, to make you talk.

01:07:38.340 --> 01:07:41.040
Shelby, tell me, why did you go to--

01:07:41.040 --> 01:07:43.980
Excuse me.

01:07:43.980 --> 01:07:49.040
Why did you go to the cottage last night?

01:07:49.040 --> 01:07:51.290
Well, don't you know?

01:07:51.290 --> 01:07:53.290
I was afraid you wouldn't think of hiding that shotgun.

01:07:53.290 --> 01:07:55.220
What shotgun?

01:07:55.220 --> 01:07:58.450
The one I gave you.

01:07:58.450 --> 01:08:00.000
You don't have to lie to me, darling.

01:08:00.670 --> 01:08:01.670
I'll stand by you.

01:08:06.960 --> 01:08:10.800
What's the matter, Laura?

01:08:10.800 --> 01:08:14.240
I guess I'm just nervous, that's all.

01:08:14.240 --> 01:08:16.500
So am I.

01:08:16.500 --> 01:08:20.300
First one, suspect Shelby.

01:08:20.300 --> 01:08:22.760
He seems to suspect me too.

01:08:22.760 --> 01:08:26.510
So do some of my friends.

01:08:26.510 --> 01:08:27.430
You?

01:08:27.430 --> 01:08:30.000
Don't be absurd, you could never do a thing like that.

01:08:30.920 --> 01:08:33.690
- And Shelby? - Oh, I don't think he did it.

01:08:33.870 --> 01:08:36.980
But he's capable of it.

01:08:37.910 --> 01:08:40.170
He was interested in McPherson as he is in you.

01:08:40.430 --> 01:08:42.440
But, Anne, I only met him last night.

01:08:43.050 --> 01:08:45.150
It's more than long enough sometimes.

01:08:45.250 --> 01:08:48.760
Anyway, he's better for you than Shelby. Anybody is.

01:08:49.910 --> 01:08:53.030
- Shelby's better for me. - Why?

01:08:53.430 --> 01:08:56.900
'Cause I can afford him and understand him.

01:08:57.390 --> 01:08:59.000
He's no good, but he's what I want.

01:09:00.480 --> 01:09:03.150
I'm not a nice person, Laura, neither is he.

01:09:03.150 --> 01:09:06.780
He knows I know he is just what he is.

01:09:06.780 --> 01:09:09.610
He also knows that I don't care.

01:09:09.610 --> 01:09:14.040
We belong together because we're both weak and can't seem to help it.

01:09:14.040 --> 01:09:17.380
That's why I know he's capable of murder.

01:09:17.380 --> 01:09:20.640
He's like me.

01:09:20.640 --> 01:09:25.760
No, dear, I didn't, but I thought of it.

01:09:48.360 --> 01:09:49.140
- For you, Lieutenant.

01:09:49.140 --> 01:09:53.130
- I see.

01:09:53.130 --> 01:09:56.370
- McPherson.

01:09:56.370 --> 01:09:58.320
- Yeah, I know.

01:09:58.320 --> 01:10:00.000
Don't worry, I told you I'd bring in

01:10:00.000 --> 01:10:07.210
to kill her today. Yeah, I was just going to make the arrest when you called. No, I can't tell you

01:10:07.210 --> 01:10:14.570
now. I'm not alone. You'll see when I come in. Right. See you later.

01:10:35.820 --> 01:10:38.520
All right, let's go.

01:11:00.320 --> 01:11:02.050
- Yes, sir?

01:11:02.050 --> 01:11:07.180
- Darling, accuse you.

01:11:07.180 --> 01:11:08.950
We'll fight them.

01:11:08.950 --> 01:11:13.220
I have every weapon, money, connections, prestige,

01:11:13.220 --> 01:11:14.650
and my column.

01:11:14.650 --> 01:11:16.110
Every day, millions will read about you

01:11:16.110 --> 01:11:18.140
and rally to your defense.

01:11:18.140 --> 01:11:20.940
- You talk as if you wanted to see her tried for murder.

01:11:20.940 --> 01:11:24.300
- Yes, rather than let you blacken her name

01:11:24.300 --> 01:11:28.460
with suspicions and rumors, try to prove her guilty.

01:11:28.460 --> 01:11:30.000
Get on the witness stand with your Porsche,

01:11:30.000 --> 01:11:33.200
I'll expose your cheap method you used on her.

01:11:33.200 --> 01:11:34.200
Thank you, Bessie.

01:11:34.200 --> 01:11:38.690
Laura, I told you to watch out for this fellow.

01:11:38.690 --> 01:11:39.690
Can we go now?

01:11:39.690 --> 01:11:40.670
I warned you.

01:11:40.670 --> 01:11:45.830
It's too bad you didn't open that door Friday night, Carpenter.

01:11:45.830 --> 01:11:46.980
Wait a minute.

01:11:47.980 --> 01:11:48.980
Shelby!

01:11:50.990 --> 01:11:53.770
Oh, did he hurt you, darling?

01:11:53.770 --> 01:11:55.050
Darling!

01:11:55.050 --> 01:11:58.930
Anne?

01:12:04.060 --> 01:12:07.400
Come in. Sit down.

01:12:09.780 --> 01:12:11.460
- It'll be all, Gallagher. - Yes.

01:12:11.590 --> 01:12:19.610
Let's have it. Look at me.

01:12:21.710 --> 01:12:24.130
What are you trying to do? Force a confession out of me?

01:12:24.450 --> 01:12:26.340
You've been holding out and I want to know why.

01:12:27.170 --> 01:12:28.930
It'll be easier for you if you tell the truth.

01:12:30.510 --> 01:12:33.000
- What difference does it make what I say?

01:12:33.000 --> 01:12:35.280
You've made up your mind I'm guilty.

01:12:35.280 --> 01:12:37.320
- Are you?

01:12:37.320 --> 01:12:39.090
- Don't tell me you have any doubts.

01:12:39.090 --> 01:12:42.350
Since you can't, please.

01:12:42.350 --> 01:12:49.130
Do I have to have those lights in my face?

01:12:49.130 --> 01:12:52.340
- Thanks.

01:12:52.340 --> 01:12:57.970
No, I didn't kill Diane Redfern or anyone else.

01:12:57.970 --> 01:12:59.000
- Then why?

01:13:00.570 --> 01:13:04.770
- Why did you tell me the radio at your country place was broken? - Because it was broken.

01:13:04.850 --> 01:13:09.120
- Not when I tried it. - Just as I was leaving the village, I asked the local handyman to fix it.

01:13:09.120 --> 01:13:13.160
- How did he get in? - I always leave a key under the flower pot on the porch.

01:13:13.220 --> 01:13:16.930
You're too intelligent to make up something I could check so easily.

01:13:16.930 --> 01:13:21.830
But you're intelligent enough to have broken it yourself to strengthen your story.

01:13:21.970 --> 01:13:26.630
The main thing I want to know is why you pulled that switch on me about Carpenter.

01:13:26.780 --> 01:13:29.480
You told me last night you decided not to marry him.

01:13:30.830 --> 01:13:34.520
- Yes, I guess I did. - But the day it was on again. Why?

01:13:35.090 --> 01:13:37.680
Well, I... I changed my mind.

01:13:39.550 --> 01:13:40.950
What are you trying to hide?

01:13:41.380 --> 01:13:43.680
Don't you realize you're involved in the murder?

01:13:44.110 --> 01:13:47.060
You've got yourself in a jam that's not going to be easy to get out of.

01:13:47.110 --> 01:13:48.810
Unless you're on the level with me.

01:13:49.270 --> 01:13:50.940
This is no time for secrets.

01:13:51.820 --> 01:13:53.730
Now, did you really decide to call it off?

01:13:53.980 --> 01:13:55.420
Or did you just tell me that?

01:13:55.500 --> 01:13:57.200
Because you knew I wanted to hear it.

01:14:02.040 --> 01:14:05.260
What went on between you and Carpenter when you saw him last night?

01:14:05.520 --> 01:14:07.490
Did he persuade you to make up?

01:14:08.950 --> 01:14:11.140
Or did you agree to pretend you had?

01:14:11.210 --> 01:14:13.440
- Was that it? - Well, we...

01:14:13.700 --> 01:14:15.920
- That is, both of us thought... - He convinced you

01:14:16.080 --> 01:14:19.420
that if you broke the engagement now, people would think you believed he was guilty.

01:14:20.240 --> 01:14:23.760
Yes. Now I know it was only because he thought I was.

01:14:23.920 --> 01:14:26.760
- Did you believe he was guilty? - No, I'm sure he isn't.

01:14:27.050 --> 01:14:29.590
But he'd gotten himself into an awfully suspicious position.

01:14:30.000 --> 01:14:32.960
And he's the sort of man that people are always ready to believe the worst about.

01:14:34.880 --> 01:14:42.850
- Are you in love with him? - I don't see how I ever could have been.

01:14:47.950 --> 01:14:52.960
- Come on, you're going home. - I thought I was...

01:14:53.040 --> 01:14:56.880
That's what I wanted you to think. You and a few others.

01:14:57.500 --> 01:15:00.000
- I didn't even book you. - You mean there was somebody...

01:15:00.000 --> 01:15:02.180
I was 99% certain about you,

01:15:02.180 --> 01:15:04.710
but I had to get rid of that 1% doubt.

01:15:04.710 --> 01:15:07.170
Wasn't there an easier way to make sure?

01:15:07.170 --> 01:15:11.550
Reached a point where I needed official surroundings.

01:15:11.550 --> 01:15:13.480
Then it was worth it, Mark.

01:15:13.480 --> 01:15:21.630
I'll call a cab before you.

01:15:36.520 --> 01:15:38.650
- Good night, I'll see you tomorrow.

01:15:38.650 --> 01:15:41.960
- Good night.

01:15:41.960 --> 01:15:45.360
- I'm going over to Lydek's apartment.

01:17:07.000 --> 01:17:08.870
It still doesn't make sense to me, Laura.

01:17:09.020 --> 01:17:12.890
- He's playing some sort of a game with you. - I don't think so.

01:17:12.940 --> 01:17:17.390
I don't deny that he's infatuated with you in some warped way of his own.

01:17:18.120 --> 01:17:21.670
But he isn't capable of any normal, warm, human relationship.

01:17:22.200 --> 01:17:24.480
He's been dealing with criminals too long.

01:17:26.680 --> 01:17:29.470
Only you were unattainable when he thought you were dead.

01:17:30.000 --> 01:17:36.160
when he wanted you most. But he was glad when I came back, as if he were waiting for me. Do you know

01:17:36.160 --> 01:17:43.500
what he calls women? Dames. A dame in Washington Heights got a fox fur out of him. His very words.

01:17:44.330 --> 01:17:52.290
That doesn't mean anything. He isn't like that. Laura, you have one tragic weakness. With you, a lean,

01:17:52.490 --> 01:18:00.000
strong body is the measure of a man, and you always get hurt. No man is ever going to hurt me again.

01:18:01.120 --> 01:18:14.840
no one not even you i hurt you laura look at me when a man has everything in the world that he wants

01:18:14.840 --> 01:18:22.320
except what he wants most he loses his self-respect it makes him bitter laura

01:18:22.320 --> 01:18:30.000
he wants to hurt someone as he's been hurt you were a long time in

01:18:30.060 --> 01:18:35.170
Finding out about Shelby, but that's over now.

01:18:36.570 --> 01:18:38.270
We'll be back together again.

01:18:40.490 --> 01:18:43.510
Haven't you heard of science's newest triumph, the doorbell?

01:18:45.430 --> 01:18:50.490
I don't like to remind it. That was the murderer's signal.

01:18:51.030 --> 01:18:53.250
Did you eavesdrop too? I hope.

01:18:54.120 --> 01:18:56.320
I thought you'd like to know. We tested your shotgun.

01:18:56.640 --> 01:18:57.430
It isn't the one.

01:18:58.230 --> 01:19:00.000
Now, that's what I call a typical move.

01:19:00.640 --> 01:19:02.720
A real key to the man's character.

01:19:03.530 --> 01:19:05.960
First, he tells you that he thinks you're innocent,

01:19:06.060 --> 01:19:07.750
then proceeds to check up on you.

01:19:07.980 --> 01:19:10.310
I report that I think she's innocent.

01:19:10.340 --> 01:19:11.970
That's my own personal opinion.

01:19:12.300 --> 01:19:15.240
When I submit proof, it becomes the opinion of the department.

01:19:15.330 --> 01:19:17.770
This entire maneuver could be a trick to get you off your guard.

01:19:17.870 --> 01:19:19.620
It could be, but it isn't.

01:19:19.900 --> 01:19:21.360
I believe you, Mark.

01:19:21.740 --> 01:19:23.810
It's the same obvious pattern, Laura.

01:19:24.210 --> 01:19:27.650
If McPherson weren't muscular and handsome in a cheap sort of way,

01:19:27.750 --> 01:19:29.600
you'd see through him in a second.

01:19:30.000 --> 01:19:33.330
- Waldo, I mean to be as kind about this as I know how,

01:19:33.330 --> 01:19:35.010
but I must tell you,

01:19:35.010 --> 01:19:38.190
you're the one who follows the same obvious pattern.

01:19:38.190 --> 01:19:39.730
First it was Jacoby, then Shelby,

01:19:39.730 --> 01:19:40.830
and now I suppose-- - It's all right.

01:19:40.830 --> 01:19:42.940
- I don't think we should see each other again.

01:19:42.940 --> 01:19:45.210
- You're not yourself, darling.

01:19:45.210 --> 01:19:46.470
- Yes, I am.

01:19:46.470 --> 01:19:52.780
For the first time in ages, I know what I'm doing.

01:19:52.780 --> 01:19:55.090
- Very well.

01:19:55.090 --> 01:19:57.450
I hope you'll never regret what promises

01:19:57.450 --> 01:20:00.000
to be a disgustingly earthy relationship.

01:20:02.300 --> 01:20:04.130
My congratulations, Macpherson.

01:20:04.930 --> 01:20:07.590
Listen to my broadcast in 15 minutes.

01:20:08.060 --> 01:20:10.920
I'm discussing great lovers of history.

01:20:32.010 --> 01:20:38.360
- It was the most difficult thing I ever had to do in my whole life. - All I need is the gun.

01:20:38.360 --> 01:20:46.710
- What are you doing? - Do you know the combination to this thing?

01:20:46.710 --> 01:20:51.890
- I knew it had one. - Must be somewhere.

01:21:02.970 --> 01:21:07.190
- Have you ever seen this before? - No.

01:21:07.190 --> 01:21:13.400
- Waldo gave you that clock, didn't he? - Yes.

01:21:13.400 --> 01:21:19.970
The doorbell rang and Diane Redfern went to the door and you're negligee.

01:21:19.970 --> 01:21:25.040
She opened the door. The room was dark.

01:21:25.040 --> 01:21:30.000
Waldo saw a girl standing there and he assumed it was you. He figured that if he

01:21:30.000 --> 01:21:33.000
He couldn't have you himself. He was going to make sure that nobody else did.

01:21:33.390 --> 01:21:34.390
So he let her have it.

01:21:36.700 --> 01:21:40.830
Put both barrels right in the face.

01:21:43.490 --> 01:21:44.490
She fell here.

01:21:46.320 --> 01:21:48.320
Waldo heard Shelby running from the next room.

01:21:49.190 --> 01:21:51.190
So he hid in a stairway outside.

01:21:53.500 --> 01:21:54.500
Shelby was scared.

01:21:54.960 --> 01:21:56.390
So he ran out as fast as he could.

01:21:56.390 --> 01:21:59.250
Then Waldo came back and placed the gun on that clock.

01:22:02.010 --> 01:22:05.470
I knew it. I felt it ever since I came back.

01:22:05.510 --> 01:22:07.920
I didn't want to believe it.

01:22:09.090 --> 01:22:13.320
I couldn't make myself believe that Waldo was a murderer.

01:22:13.350 --> 01:22:18.780
- Well, he is. - He didn't really kill Diane Redfern.

01:22:18.860 --> 01:22:20.770
- I killed her. - What are you talking about?

01:22:20.910 --> 01:22:22.530
But I did, Mark.

01:22:22.830 --> 01:22:25.650
I did as surely as if I'd pulled the trigger myself.

01:22:25.730 --> 01:22:27.400
That's nonsense. Forget it.

01:22:27.880 --> 01:22:30.000
No, Mark, I can't. I'm as guilty as I can.

01:22:30.000 --> 01:22:35.020
for anything I did, for what I didn't do.

01:22:35.120 --> 01:22:40.440
But I couldn't help myself. I owed him too much.

01:22:40.540 --> 01:22:47.540
You can understand that, Laura.

01:22:47.640 --> 01:22:50.370
But I can't understand why you've tried so hard

01:22:50.470 --> 01:22:52.270
to protect Shelby these last few days.

01:22:52.370 --> 01:22:54.270
I was nearly frantic for fear you'd arrest Shelby.

01:22:54.370 --> 01:22:55.780
I knew he wasn't guilty.

01:22:55.880 --> 01:22:57.700
He hasn't enough courage to kill a fly.

01:22:57.800 --> 01:23:00.000
And Waldo was doing everything he could do

01:23:00.000 --> 01:23:05.180
could to incriminate him. It was his way of getting rid of Shelby, just as he did Jathaby.

01:23:08.900 --> 01:23:13.060
I must say, for a charming, intelligent girl, you certainly surrounded yourself with a remarkable

01:23:13.060 --> 01:23:21.580
collection of dopes. Don't touch anything. Fingerprints will be important. I'll have

01:23:21.580 --> 01:23:25.230
the clock picked up tomorrow. What are you going to do? Arrest Waldo.

01:23:25.460 --> 01:23:27.820
Come on. Can't be helped.

01:23:34.760 --> 01:23:37.480
- That's to make sure you're not disturbed.

01:23:37.480 --> 01:23:40.100
If the doorbell rings, don't answer it.

01:23:40.100 --> 01:23:41.600
I'll phone you in the morning.

01:23:41.600 --> 01:23:43.920
- Good night, Mark.

01:23:43.920 --> 01:23:45.160
- Get some sleep.

01:23:45.160 --> 01:23:46.900
Forget the whole thing like a bad dream.

01:25:39.470 --> 01:25:43.060
And thus, as history has proved, love is eternal.

01:25:43.920 --> 01:25:49.170
It has been the strongest motivation for human actions throughout centuries.

01:25:49.760 --> 01:25:54.870
Love is stronger than life. It reaches beyond the dark shadow of death.

01:25:55.240 --> 01:26:00.000
I close this evening's broadcast with some favorite lines from Darwin.

01:26:00.000 --> 01:26:08.390
Brief life, they are not long, the weeping and the laughter,

01:26:08.390 --> 01:26:12.160
love and desire and hate.

01:26:12.160 --> 01:26:17.850
I think they have no portion in us after we pass the gate.

01:26:17.850 --> 01:26:22.360
- Who's tailing Lidecker? - I was going to when he came out.

01:26:22.360 --> 01:26:25.270
- He left five minutes ago. - He didn't come out this way.

01:26:25.270 --> 01:26:28.130
Must have gone out the back way. Come on, let's check.

01:26:30.000 --> 01:26:35.120
They are not long, days of wine and roses.

01:26:35.120 --> 01:26:39.610
Out of a mystery dream our path emerges for a while,

01:26:39.610 --> 01:26:43.640
then closes within a dream.

01:26:43.640 --> 01:26:45.640
That's the way it is, isn't it, Laura?

01:26:45.640 --> 01:26:48.560
You have heard the voice of Waldo Lidecker,

01:26:48.560 --> 01:26:50.740
by electrical transcription.

01:26:50.740 --> 01:26:54.560
Waldo, you've taken one life. Isn't that another?

01:26:54.560 --> 01:26:58.230
The best part of myself, that's what you are.

01:26:58.230 --> 01:27:00.000
Do you think...

01:27:00.000 --> 01:27:05.800
Do you think I'm going to leave it to the vulgar pawing of a second-rate detective who thinks you're a dame?

01:27:05.880 --> 01:27:12.110
Do you think I could bear the thought of him holding you in his arms, kissing you, loving you?

01:27:12.190 --> 01:27:15.360
- Laura! It's Mark! Open the door! - There he is now.

01:27:15.440 --> 01:27:20.270
- Laura! - He'll find us together, Laura.

01:27:20.350 --> 01:27:23.190
As we always have been, as we always should be, as we always will be.

01:27:24.230 --> 01:27:25.320
Go back!
